<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seuthes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 424–c. 410 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seuthes I was king of the Odrysian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> from 424 BCE, succeeding his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitalces">Sitalces</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that under him the yearly tribute from the Thracian tribes and the Greek cities reached about 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, with gifts of equal value, the height of Odrysian wealth. He married Stratonice, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas-ii-of-macedon">Perdiccas II of Macedon</a>.</p></div>
